One model defines the editor screens, validation rules, Astro collections, TypeScript types, and file layout.

## Collections and singletons

A collection contains many entries. Each collection has one slug field that identifies an entry in the repository and on the website. A singleton is one settings document, such as navigation or site metadata.

```ts
export const site = defineSite({
  root: "./content",
  collections: {
    projects: collection({
      label: "Projects",
      sortable: true,
      fields: {
        title: text({ required: true, listed: true }),
        slug: slug({ from: "title" }),
        summary: richText(),
        gallery: media({ multiple: true, accept: "image" }),
      },
    }),
  },
});
```

## Field types

Mica includes text, textarea, rich text, number, boolean, date, email, URL, slug, select, object, repeater, relation, media, color, and coordinates fields.

Each field can include an editor label, help text, group, width, visibility, and required state. Specific fields add rules such as length, number range, accepted media, or select options.

## The file layout

Entries with only structured fields use one JSON file. Rich text and media need a directory with an `index.json` file.

```text
content/
├── projects/
│   ├── _order.json
│   └── house-a/
│       ├── index.json
│       ├── summary.mdoc
│       └── media/exterior.jpg
└── navigation.json
```

Keys stay in model order. A repeat save of the same values gives the same bytes. This keeps the Git diff focused.

## Change the model

Run an Astro build after each model change and commit the new `.mica/schema.json`. A new required field makes old entries invalid until an editor adds the value. The build reports this problem before deployment.

